\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The topology of real fields}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{For a finite set #{V}, put #{X_V=\operatorname{Map}(V,\mathbb R)}, the
[real vertex-field space](fdip-0002). Its indicator fields #{\delta_v}
form the [finite function basis](fdip-0010), so
#{x=\sum_{v\in V}x(v)\delta_v}. Use the [counting inner product](fdip-001O)
#{h_V(x,y)=\sum_vx(v)y(v)} and its [perfect duality](fdip-001F).
The carrier #{V} is finite; the set #{X_V} of configurations is generally
infinite. For #{V=\varnothing}, #{X_V=\{0\}}.}
\p{The \newvocab{real field topology} is the finite product of the usual
topology on #{\mathbb R}. Its basic open sets are boxes
#{\prod_{v\in V}O_v} with every #{O_v} open.}
\p{For a topological space #{Y}, a map #{f:Y\to X_V} is continuous
if and only if every coordinate #{y\mapsto f(y)(v)} is continuous.
Consequently every real linear map between finite field spaces is
continuous, and every linear isomorphism is a homeomorphism.}
\proof{
\p{Evaluation #{x\mapsto x(v)} is continuous by the product topology.
Conversely, the inverse image of a basic box is the finite intersection
#{\bigcap_v\{y:f(y)(v)\in O_v\}}, which is open if all coordinates are
continuous. For a linear map #{T:X_V\to X_W}, the identity
#{(Tx)(w)=\sum_vx(v)(T\delta_v)(w)} makes each output coordinate a
finite linear combination of continuous inputs. Apply this also to the
inverse when #{T} is invertible. The empty product has the single basic
box #{X_\varnothing}; the same statements hold directly.}
\p{Pointwise addition, scalar multiplication and #{h_V} are continuous
by finite sums and products of coordinates. The counting form is positive:
if #{x\ne0}, some #{x(v)^2>0}. Its perfectness can also be read directly:
for a linear functional #{\ell}, put #{a(v)=\ell(\delta_v)}; then
#{\ell(x)=h_V(a,x)}, uniquely by evaluation on the indicators. These are
the real specializations of the cited field and duality constructions.}
}
